# Dual-use disclosure

Package: socket
Content policy class: dual-use (https://docs.npmjs.com/policies/dual-use)

This package is a small launcher for the Socket CLI, a security research
tool. Installing it adds one command, socket. The launcher does not contain
the tool itself: it picks the platform-specific optional dependency that
matches the user's operating system and CPU (the @socketbin/cli-* and
@socketsecurity/cli.exe.* packages listed in this manifest) and runs the
compiled Socket CLI binary that package ships.

## What the tool does that can look like malware

The Socket CLI wraps package-manager commands, reads dependency trees and
project files such as manifests and lockfiles, sends package names,
versions, and user-requested scan files to the socket.dev security API, and
can warn about or refuse to continue an install when a dependency looks
risky. The user's API token is sent to authenticate those requests. A
launcher that selects and executes a compiled binary from an optional
dependency is also a pattern automated scanning treats with suspicion.

## Intended legitimate use

The tool exists for defensive supply-chain security: developers and CI
systems use it to find known-malicious, hijacked, typosquatted, or
policy-violating dependencies before those dependencies run. The tool runs
only when a user or a CI job invokes it. Its source code is public at
https://github.com/SocketDev/socket-cli and issues are tracked there.
